WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.560 --> 00:00:02.770
Everything's personal in Ant-Man and the Wasp.

00:00:02.770 --> 00:00:07.560
The small-scale superhero movie is a light
and summery story with no real villains, just

00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:11.630
characters trying to get things done and get
home, sometimes at odds with each other.

00:00:11.630 --> 00:00:15.411
But while the Ant-Man sequel's stakes may
be small, the characters themselves have long

00:00:15.411 --> 00:00:19.539
histories that stretch back decades, and we're
not just talking about the two with their

00:00:19.539 --> 00:00:20.580
names in the title.

00:00:20.580 --> 00:00:24.249
There's more going on with some of these supporting
characters than you may have noticed, and

00:00:24.249 --> 00:00:27.019
they may affect the future of the entire MCU.

00:00:27.019 --> 00:00:30.829
When it comes to Ant-Man and the Wasp, these
are the characters that deserve your extra

00:00:30.829 --> 00:00:31.869
attention.

00:00:31.869 --> 00:00:33.950
Cassie Lang

00:00:33.950 --> 00:00:37.730
Scott Lang's daughter Cassie is Ant-Man and
the Wasp's secret MVP.

00:00:37.730 --> 00:00:41.500
In one of the movie's most adorable moments,
the 10-year-old makes it clear to her dad

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:43.780
that she wants to be his superhero sidekick.

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.090
It's a suggestion that Scott brushes off,
at least, for now.

00:00:47.090 --> 00:00:51.390
In the comics, Cassie does get in on the superhero
game, working both on her own and with the

00:00:51.390 --> 00:00:54.460
Young Avengers under the names of Stature
and Stinger.

00:00:54.460 --> 00:00:58.530
Like her dad, she has size-changing abilities,
gaining the power to do so without the help

00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:01.990
of any technology through her lifetime exposure
to Pym particles.

00:01:01.990 --> 00:01:05.150
So why does Cassie's comic book history matter
to the MCU?

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:10.240
Well, Marvel Studios president Kevin Feige
has openly discussed how the company is "planting

00:01:10.240 --> 00:01:13.850
seeds" for the Young Avengers' potential arrival
in the movies, saying,

00:01:13.850 --> 00:01:17.060
"That's why we wanted Cassie [...] inspired
by her father."

00:01:17.060 --> 00:01:21.530
You may see that inspiration pay off sooner
than you think, as Marvel has reportedly cast

00:01:21.530 --> 00:01:25.210
an older actress to play Cassie in the fourth
Avengers movie.

00:01:25.210 --> 00:01:28.340
Is this aspiring heroine about to get her
time in the spotlight?

00:01:28.340 --> 00:01:30.909
It certainly seems that way.

00:01:30.909 --> 00:01:32.479
Elihas Starr

00:01:32.479 --> 00:01:36.909
Elihas Starr only appears in flashback in
the Ant-Man sequel, being the bald man who

00:01:36.909 --> 00:01:40.219
gets blown up in a quantum experiment as part
of Ghost's origin story.

00:01:40.219 --> 00:01:45.119
But while he may be a sympathetic figure in
the movie, his comic book namesake is anything

00:01:45.119 --> 00:01:46.229
but.

00:01:46.229 --> 00:01:51.399
Introduced in a 1962 issue of Tales to Astonish,
Elihas is a brainiac villain appropriately

00:01:51.399 --> 00:01:56.320
known as Egghead, a super genius who becomes
obsessed with defeating Ant-Man and the Avengers.

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:58.829
You don't really get any of that in the movie,
though.

00:01:58.829 --> 00:02:03.829
Instead, Elihas' animosity toward Pym is transferred
to his daughter Ava, aka Ghost, who has a

00:02:03.829 --> 00:02:08.040
reflexive distaste for Pym due to what she
sees as his part in her parents' deaths.

00:02:08.040 --> 00:02:13.360
That fatherly aspect to Egghead is entirely
an invention of the MCU; in the comics, Ava

00:02:13.360 --> 00:02:14.659
doesn't exist.

00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.320
Speaking of which…

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:17.320
Ghost

00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:21.280
Ghost, is one of the more mysterious characters
in Ant-Man and the Wasp.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:26.319
With no time for chit-chat, she's got an understandable
focus on staying alive and finding a cure

00:02:26.319 --> 00:02:29.620
for her uncontrollable phasing condition before
it tears her apart.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:34.290
Motivated by survival, Ava's the most sympathetic
villain the MCU has seen yet.

00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:38.540
By the time the credits roll and Ghost is
cured, Scott's casually referring to Ava as

00:02:38.540 --> 00:02:42.510
the team's "new Ghost friend", making her
story feel less like a villain's tale and

00:02:42.510 --> 00:02:45.030
more like a backdoor pilot for a whole new
hero.

00:02:45.030 --> 00:02:48.280
In MCU territory, they call that move "the
Bucky Barnes."

00:02:48.280 --> 00:02:51.390
In the comics, Ghost is not named Ava, nor
is she a she.

00:02:51.390 --> 00:02:55.450
The comic book Ghost, a man whose real name
we never learn, was introduced as a villain

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:57.939
in a 1987 issue of Iron Man.

00:02:57.939 --> 00:03:02.079
Eventually, the character joins the Thunderbolts,
a team of bad guys gone good.

00:03:02.079 --> 00:03:05.709
Since Ghost survives Ant-Man and the Wasp,
fans are chattering about the possibility

00:03:05.709 --> 00:03:09.110
of the anti-hero eventually joining a movie
version of the Thunderbolts.

00:03:09.110 --> 00:03:13.220
While we don't know for sure if that's happening,
some of the pieces are in place.

00:03:13.220 --> 00:03:17.440
In the comics, the team was originally led
by Baron Zemo, a character who appeared in

00:03:17.440 --> 00:03:20.739
- and survived - Captain America: Civil War.

00:03:20.739 --> 00:03:22.400
Jimmy Woo

00:03:22.400 --> 00:03:26.930
Would you believe that FBI Special Agent James
"Jimmy" Woo is the Ant-Man and the Wasp character

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:29.000
with the longest comic book history?

00:03:29.000 --> 00:03:34.140
With the character first introduced in 1956,
it might also explain why he's such an unabashed

00:03:34.140 --> 00:03:35.140
square.

00:03:35.140 --> 00:03:39.239
Woo is such an old-fashioned character that
he predates Marvel Comics as a company, first

00:03:39.239 --> 00:03:43.689
appearing in an Atlas Comics series about
a Chinese supervillain named "The Yellow Claw."

00:03:43.689 --> 00:03:45.830
A name which aged very poorly!

00:03:45.830 --> 00:03:46.830
"Hey!

00:03:46.830 --> 00:03:47.340
It's complicated."

00:03:47.340 --> 00:03:47.900
"It is?"

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:48.900
"It's complicated."

00:03:48.980 --> 00:03:52.920
When Atlas turned into Marvel, Woo eventually
ended up repackaged as a S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:03:53.080 --> 00:03:54.080
agent.

00:03:54.090 --> 00:03:58.010
But Marvel never forgot his Atlas Comics roots,
eventually making him the center of a new

00:03:58.010 --> 00:04:03.950
team called the Agents of Atlas in 2006, a
squad composed of retro heroes from the '40s

00:04:03.950 --> 00:04:05.030
and '50s.

00:04:05.030 --> 00:04:09.239
Woo's quaint style in Ant-Man and the Wasp
seems like a reference to his origins in another

00:04:09.239 --> 00:04:10.239
era.

00:04:10.239 --> 00:04:14.390
Being a stickler for the rules makes him a
natural heel in a world where Scott Lang - technically

00:04:14.390 --> 00:04:18.489
a criminal! - can be among the ranks of the
world's mightiest heroes.

00:04:18.489 --> 00:04:20.729
Whale Boat Captain Daniel Gooobler

00:04:20.729 --> 00:04:25.210
The whale boat captain played by comedian
Tim Heidecker is not a comic book character,

00:04:25.210 --> 00:04:28.330
but rather a manifestation of a real-life
inside joke.

00:04:28.330 --> 00:04:33.250
It all goes back to the satirical movie review
series On Cinema at the Cinema, starring Heidecker

00:04:33.250 --> 00:04:38.540
and comedian Gregg Turkington - a show on
which Ant-Man director Peyton Reed once appeared.

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:42.990
Ever since Turkington cameoed in the first
Ant-Man as Scott's Baskin-Robbins manager,

00:04:42.990 --> 00:04:46.900
the two co-hosts have been locked in a rivalry
over which of them can appear in more Marvel

00:04:46.900 --> 00:04:52.740
movies - a competition Tim tied in 2015 by
appearing in Fox's Fantastic Four reboot.

00:04:52.740 --> 00:04:56.630
"I completely understand why you would want
to be involved in it, why you would pay the

00:04:56.630 --> 00:04:59.440
$15,000 to cameo in the movie."

00:04:59.440 --> 00:05:00.260
"No.

00:05:00.260 --> 00:05:01.120
No."

00:05:01.280 --> 00:05:04.200
Now that Tim's lunged into the lead with this
appearance in Ant-Man and the Wasp, the tables

00:05:04.860 --> 00:05:06.510
have turned on Turkington.

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:10.280
The appearance makes for one of the movie's
most amusing gags, provided you know about

00:05:10.280 --> 00:05:12.820
the long-running inside joke.

00:05:12.820 --> 00:05:14.530
Bill Foster

00:05:14.530 --> 00:05:18.560
Laurence Fishburne's Bill Foster serves as
an Ant-Man and the Wasp antagonist for about

00:05:18.560 --> 00:05:23.380
five minutes, with any villainy on his part
coming down to a difference in perspectives.

00:05:23.380 --> 00:05:27.680
The professor is introduced as a former partner
of Pym's who worked alongside him on a project

00:05:27.680 --> 00:05:28.960
called Goliath.

00:05:28.960 --> 00:05:33.300
But what's never made too clear is that Goliath
is a former superhero alter-ego of Hank's,

00:05:33.300 --> 00:05:36.229
and that - for a while - Foster was Goliath.

00:05:36.229 --> 00:05:40.139
After parting ways with Pym, Foster gained
the power to grow to great heights himself

00:05:40.139 --> 00:05:42.310
through his tinkering with Pym particles.

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:48.020
He then started a blaxploitation-era crime-fighting
career as the hero Black Goliath in a 1975

00:05:48.020 --> 00:05:49.020
issue of Power-Man.

00:05:49.020 --> 00:05:54.080
Later, after using the name Giant-Man for
a while, Foster would just take the name Goliath.

00:05:54.080 --> 00:05:58.910
While Foster never goes gigantic in the Ant-Man
sequel, he definitely has the ability to,

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:00.729
which may come in handy in the future.

00:06:00.729 --> 00:06:03.569
This is assuming Bill survived the movie's
credit scene.

00:06:03.569 --> 00:06:07.630
With one Ant-Man and two Wasps snapped out
of existence, the remaining heroes probably

00:06:07.630 --> 00:06:09.530
need all the help they can get.

00:06:09.530 --> 00:06:10.850
Thanks for watching!

00:06:10.850 --> 00:06:13.789
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our
YouTube channel.

00:06:13.789 --> 00:06:15.890
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know
you'll love, too!

